cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "AetherFlow: Automated Post-Merge Verification and Dynamic Conflict Resolution in Multi-Agent Programming Environments"
message: "If you use this research, data, or code, please cite as below."
type: report
version: "1.0.0"
date-released: "2026-11-05"
license: MIT
abstract: |
  AetherFlow is a formal architecture that replaces pre-merge gating
  with three composable mechanisms: (i) lock-free merge entry, in
  which changesets are appended to the trunk log without waiting for
  verification; (ii) asynchronous post-merge verification, computed by
  a recursive operator that attributes failures to the changeset that
  introduced them and marks descendants as tainted rather than failed;
  and (iii) proactive remediation by agents under a graded escalation
  policy.

  Three definitions are given: a dynamic conflict window W(c_i, c_j);
  the recursive verification operator ⊕_V; and a bound
  P(Remediation) ≥ 1 − (λ_fail/μ_remediate)^K · e^(−γ·T_react).

  Three propositions are proven: admission independence, monotonicity
  of the conflict window, and a closed-form minimum tier depth for any
  target residual risk.

  AetherFlow is the integration-layer complement to the AetherGit
  storage architecture. The paper reports no implementation,
  simulation, or benchmark.
keywords:
  - source control
  - merge queues
  - agentic software engineering
  - post-merge verification
  - auto-remediation
  - conflict resolution
  - distributed systems
  - formal methods
  - aetherflow
